1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,200
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,440 --> 00:00:05,260
Before we move forward, think about Lesson
One for a moment.

3
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:09,620
What do you remember about the Comfort
Zone, the Challenge Zone,

4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:10,940
and the Pain Zone?

5
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:13,760
Which one is where growth happens?

6
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:15,940
Hold your answer.

7
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:20,260
Today we begin Unit One for real, with the
question

8
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:23,780
underneath everything else we’ll do in
this unit: who

9
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:24,980
am I?

10
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:29,000
To answer that, we need to start with one
word:

11
00:00:29,660 --> 00:00:30,860
identity.

12
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:34,840
Identity is what makes something itself,
and not something else.

13
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:39,220
Think about an ordinary object, like a
wooden table.

14
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:43,880
You can describe its shape, color,
material, marks,

15
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:45,420
and the way it was made.

16
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:47,720
Now think about a flag.

17
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,900
A flag is more than colors and shapes.

18
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:54,280
It carries meaning, history, and
belonging.

19
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:58,840
Then think about someone close to you,
like your mother.

20
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:03,280
You can probably describe what makes her
unique to you without needing a

21
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:04,480
definition first.

22
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:06,540
Objects have identity.

23
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:08,460
Places have identity.

24
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:10,500
Communities have identity.

25
00:01:10,940 --> 00:01:12,140
And you have one too.

26
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:15,020
Now turn the question toward yourself.

27
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:17,160
Who are you?

28
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:19,020
How would you describe yourself?

29
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:23,680
What makes you different from other people
your age, or other people

30
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:24,960
who have similar backgrounds?

31
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:28,060
That question can be harder than it
sounds.

32
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:32,840
Many of us spend a lot of time learning
about other people and

33
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:35,520
very little time learning how to describe
ourselves.

34
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:40,360
Self- awareness begins when you can look
at your own identity

35
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:44,140
honestly, without exaggerating your
strengths and without

36
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:45,340
talking yourself down.

37
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:49,620
The source curriculum makes a simple
point: people are

38
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:53,060
unique in many ways, including things like
fingerprints,

39
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:55,120
eye patterns, and DNA.

40
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:58,540
But identity goes far beyond biology.

41
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:02,240
It includes the experiences, values,
interests,

42
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:06,460
abilities, beliefs, relationships, and
environments that

43
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:09,800
shape how you see yourself and how you
move through the world.

44
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:14,420
A person who understands their identity
can recognize their strengths,

45
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:18,400
work on their weaknesses, and make better
choices about where they can

46
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:19,460
contribute.

47
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:23,400
One important rule before we go further:
identity is

48
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,600
not a ranking.

49
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:28,780
The color of your skin, how you look,
where you were

50
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:32,480
born, where you live, or the community you
belong to

51
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:35,760
do not make you better” or worse” than
someone else.

52
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:40,060
Difference is a real, but difference is
not superiority.

53
00:02:41,460 --> 00:02:43,660
For a community leader, this matters.

54
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:48,020
You will work with people whose
backgrounds, interests, opinions,

55
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:51,060
and experiences may be very different from
yours.

56
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:55,440
Respect starts with understanding that
difference is

57
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:56,620
normal.

58
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:57,900
Picture two photos.

59
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:01,340
In the first, a group of very young
children.

60
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:03,920
In the second, a group of adults.

61
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:08,560
Which group would usually be easier for
you to describe as

62
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:09,760
distinct individuals?

63
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:14,160
The curriculum uses this comparison to
make a point:

64
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:18,700
as we grow, more layers of identity become
visible through

65
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:22,400
our experiences, choices, roles,
interests,

66
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:23,940
and relationships.

67
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:27,040
Identity is not one fixed label.

68
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:31,600
Some parts stay with us, while other parts
become clearer, stronger,

69
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,800
or different as our lives change.

70
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:35,900
Now it’s your turn.

71
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:40,140
Use the Personal Identity Wheel provided
with this lesson, or draw a

72
00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:41,340
simple circle on paper.

73
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:43,040
Write your name in the center.

74
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:45,700
Then write three words that describe you.

75
00:03:46,340 --> 00:03:50,080
Around the wheel, add your favorite music,
a skill you’re proud of,

76
00:03:50,340 --> 00:03:54,320
your favorite movie, food, book, hobby,
color, and

77
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:56,840
a personal motto or saying you try to live
by.

78
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:00,460
Pause the video and take about five honest
minutes.

79
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:04,420
Don’t write what sounds impressive.

80
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:06,260
Write what is true.

81
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,440
Come back to your wheel and look at it as
if you were meeting

82
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:12,120
yourself for the first time.

83
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:15,700
Does one page capture your whole identity?

84
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:17,460
Of course not.

85
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:19,140
That isn’t the point.

86
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:23,180
The point is that you just practiced
something many people rarely

87
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:27,100
do on purpose: you looked directly at
yourself and

88
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:29,840
put specific parts of your identity into
words.

89
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:34,380
If you’re taking Act with other people,
compare your wheels afterward.

90
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:37,420
Notice what you share and what is
different.

91
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:41,320
Neither similarity nor difference is a
problem.

92
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:44,040
Both help you understand people better.

93
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:46,920
Now think like a community leader.

94
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:51,220
If you don’t understand your own values,
strengths, interests,

95
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:55,700
and limits, it becomes harder to
understand what role you should take in

96
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:59,540
a team or how you should respond when
someone else sees things differently.

97
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:03,840
Knowing yourself does not mean becoming
self- focused.

98
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:08,080
It gives you a stronger starting point for
serving others, because you

99
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:11,980
understand what you can offer, what you
still need to develop, and

100
00:05:11,980 --> 00:05:14,640
where another person’s strengths may
complement your own.

101
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:18,680
The source curriculum closes this lesson

102
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:22,280
by reminding us that developing ourselves

103
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:25,880
should connect to benefiting other people.

104
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:30,520
The Prophet Peace Be Upon Him said: The

105
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:34,500
most beloved people to Allah are those who
are

106
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:36,480
most beneficial to people.”

107
00:05:38,310 --> 00:05:40,090
Keep that idea with you.

108
00:05:41,150 --> 00:05:44,390
Knowing who you are is not the final

109
00:05:44,390 --> 00:05:45,590
destination.

110
00:05:45,890 --> 00:05:49,770
It is preparation for what you will do
with your abilities,

111
00:05:50,410 --> 00:05:52,450
your choices, and your time.

112
00:05:53,790 --> 00:05:57,450
The strongest identity is not the one that

113
00:05:57,450 --> 00:05:58,730
looks impressive.

114
00:05:59,190 --> 00:06:02,810
It is the one you understand well enough
to

115
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:04,390
use responsibly.

116
00:06:04,970 --> 00:06:08,930
Before we close, look back at the three
words you would have used

117
00:06:08,930 --> 00:06:11,230
to describe yourself at the start of this
lesson.

118
00:06:11,790 --> 00:06:13,410
Would you keep the same three now?

119
00:06:13,910 --> 00:06:15,130
Would you change one?

120
00:06:15,670 --> 00:06:17,930
Would you add something you had not
noticed before?

121
00:06:18,650 --> 00:06:20,470
Keep your Personal Identity Wheel.

122
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:22,070
We will build on it.

123
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:26,090
Today you moved from the general question
who am I?” to

124
00:06:26,090 --> 00:06:29,930
a real first answer: my identity is made
of many

125
00:06:29,930 --> 00:06:33,870
parts, and understanding those parts helps
me understand what

126
00:06:33,870 --> 00:06:35,110
I bring to other people.

127
00:06:35,110 --> 00:06:38,730
In the next lesson, we go from describing
your

128
00:06:38,730 --> 00:06:41,610
identity to using self- awareness on
purpose.
